@startuml keeper-sign
!theme plain
title Key keeper: generation-bound own-key signing; retirement and identity revocation are separate
' Updated: accepted architecture-review findings (2026-10-13)

participant "Agent process\n(own OS account)" as A
participant "Unix socket\n(kernel peer credentials)" as S
participant "Key keeper\n(privileged daemon)" as KK
database "Master secret\n(Secure Enclave / TPM / DPAPI)" as HS
database "Registry\n(generation-bound registrations)" as R

note over KK, R
  Integration stays disabled until registration/revocation API and
  broker migration procedure are published and qualified.
  Dispatcher §14, user manager §4.4, broker §4.3 (D-IDENTITY).
end note
A -> S: sign(bytes) with own identity
S -> KK: connection + kernel peer credentials
KK -> R: validate active ledger and registration tuple:\n(uid, generation, identity path, owner, registration generation)
R --> KK: active registration or refusal
alt unknown/stale registration or revoked identity
  KK --> A: refuse; no signature
else active registration
  KK -> HS: derive registered identity path inside the secure boundary
  HS --> KK: signing capability for this operation
  KK -> KK: sign canonical bytes with caller's own key; audit
  KK --> A: signature
end

== registration and lifecycle ==
note over KK, R
  Registration records the one-shot flag; detach before UID reuse.
  Account retirement does not itself revoke an identity.
  Only the registering dispatcher may revoke its recorded one-shot
  identity, after confirmed retirement, as a separate journaled effect.
  Unknown/refused revocation retains the cleanup obligation.
  Standing identity revocation requires an operator-signed record.
  pubkey(identity) remains a public read; no private key is returned.
  Exact registration/revocation grammar remains unpublished.
end note
note over A, KK
  Signing and decryption are audited and rate-limited.
  Decryption uses the caller's own identity.
  The agent-side caller signs its outbound exact bytes under its own UID.
  No bridge on-behalf signing operation is adopted.
end note
@enduml
